<p>
Over at <code>techhub.social</code>, lispwizard has a short post (or whatever <code>techhub.social</code> calls them) on <a href="https://techhub.social/@lispwizard/117410946106408409">saving window configurations to registers</a>. He notes how easy it is to save multiple configurations and switch between them on the fly.
</p>
<p>
Saving window configurations and other data in registers is an extraordinarily powerful technique that all Emacs users should learn and master. There&rsquo;s a problem, though, when you want to do this programmatically: which register should you use? How can you be sure that your choice won&rsquo;t conflict with a register that the user has chosen manually for some other task? There are several situations in which I want to run an Emacs function in a single window—Elfeed is an example—and I do that by saving the current window configuration, deleting the other windows, running the required function, and then restoring the previous window configuration but how do I choose a &ldquo;safe&rdquo; register to save the window configuration in?
</p>
<p>
The answer is a trick I learned from <a href="https://emacsrocks.com/">Magnar Sveen</a> years ago. Instead of using a letter to name the register, you use a symbol like <code>:saved-windows</code>. That works because when you name a register with a letter, Emacs uses a pointer to its symbol as the register name. Using a symbol name like <code>:saved-windows</code> works because the result is also a symbol pointer.
</p>
<p>
If you have the need to save something in a register programmatically, this is a good way of doing it. You don&rsquo;t have to worry about conflicts  because the user can not use symbol names for registers interactively.</p>
